  "name": "METHYLMALONIC ACID QUANTITATIVE - URINE",
  "code": "1159",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>1. Diet, nutritional status &amp; age should be considered in the evaluation of urine MMA levels.<br>2. Results should be clinically correlated as individual / biological variations can affect the test results</p>\n<p><strong>Comments</strong></p>\n<p>Elevated levels of methylmalonic acid (MMA) result from inherited defects of enzymes involved in MMA metabolism or&nbsp;inherited or acquired deficiencies of vitamin B 12 or its downstream metabolites. Of the 2, nutritional deficiencies are&nbsp;much more common and can be due to intestinal malabsorption, impaired digestion, or poor diet. MMA is a&nbsp;sensitive indicator which detects early Vitamin B 12 deficiency even when its levels are normal in serum. In pediatric&nbsp;patients, elevated MMA values indicate a probable Methylmalonic acidemia wheras in adults, it is indicative of cobalamin&nbsp;deficiency.</p>",
